# UIKit component

First check `.harness/local/skills/uikit-component.md`: if the file exists, read it before starting work. It is the project addendum — the project's own design system rules; where it disagrees with the text below, the addendum wins.

UIKit lives in `{{ uikit_dir }}` and follows the shadcn convention: a component is **copied into the repository and belongs to us**, rather than pulled in as a dependency. So it can and should be adapted to the project — but by the rules below, otherwise the set stops being a system.

## First — does the component belong here

- Does the component need a domain type, a type from the server contract, a domain word, or a business logic state? Then it is not a UIKit component but part of a screen. Its place is next to the screen.
- Does the component make sense in any other project (button, table, popover, badge)? Then it is UIKit.
- Is the component already in `{{ uikit_name }}`? Then we add nothing — we use the existing one and, if it falls short, extend it with a variant rather than cloning it.

{% if monorepo %}
UIKit depends on no other package of the repository. The check is simple: `packages/uikit/package.json` must not gain a single internal dependency.
{% else %}
UIKit imports nothing from screens or from data-handling code: `src/components/ui` imports only external packages, `@/lib/*`, and other UIKit components.
{% endif %}

## Order

1. **Install the component via the CLI.** {% if monorepo %}`cd packages/uikit && bunx shadcn@latest add <name>`{% else %}`bunx shadcn@latest add <name>` from the root{% endif %}: `components.json` is set up so the file lands in `{{ component_path }}`. A component is written by hand only when it is not in the registry.

   The set's configuration is `components.json`: style `base-nova`, component base `@base-ui/react` (not Radix), base color `zinc`, icons `@remixicon/react`, font Geist, CSS variables. The project may change it — then its `components.json` is right, not this paragraph.
2. **Variants via `cva`.** Sizes, tones, and states are described with `class-variance-authority`, not with a set of boolean props or branching in JSX. The default variant is set explicitly in `defaultVariants`.
3. **Merge classes via `cn`.** `import { cn } from "{{ utils_import }}"`. A component must accept `className` and pass it as the last argument of `cn`, otherwise it can't be customized at the call site.
4. **Color only via tokens.** Allowed are the variables from `{{ globals_css }}` and the matching Tailwind utilities (`bg-background`, `text-foreground`, `border-border`, `ring-ring`, and others from the `@theme inline` block). Literals `#rrggbb`, `rgb()`, `oklch()` and Tailwind color classes inside a component are forbidden: the dark theme breaks on them. Need a new color — add a token to both the `:root` and `.dark` blocks.
5. **Props pass through.** Extend the native element type (`React.ComponentProps<"button">`), forward `ref` and the remaining props via `...props`. A component that swallows `aria-*` and handlers is unusable.
6. **Export.** {% if monorepo %}Nothing to add: the `exports` map in `packages/uikit/package.json` exposes `./components/*` file by file.{% else %}Nothing to add: the component is imported by its file path.{% endif %} Import — `{{ component_import }}`. There is deliberately no `index.ts` barrel in this set: it would force editing the index on every `shadcn add`.

## Keyboard and accessibility

An interactive element is a `button` or `a`, not a `div` with `onClick`. Focus is visible and styled via `--ring`. Nothing is conveyed by color alone: the state is also carried by text or an icon.

## Check

```
{% if monorepo %}
bun run --filter '{{ uikit_name }}' typecheck
bunx biome check packages/uikit
bun run build
{% else %}
bun run typecheck
bunx biome check src/components/ui
bun run build
{% endif %}
```

shadcn components come formatted for prettier, while we use biome — the first `bunx biome check --write` after installing a component is normal and expected.

## What not to do

- Don't pull into UIKit a one-off component that exactly one screen needs.
- Don't create a second component instead of a variant of an existing one.
- Don't tie a component to data: it receives props, it doesn't fetch them itself.
- Don't change `components.json` for one component — it is the configuration of the whole set.
